    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0206",
      "sequence": 218,
      "meaning": "I have sung all these deeds; now hear the story that follows with attention. The wise great sage Viśvāmitra lived in a fine forest hermitage, where sages performed recitation and sacrifice but greatly feared Mārīca and Subāhu. At sight of a sacrifice the night-rangers rushed in, caused disruption, and grieved the sages. Anxiety seized Gādhi’s son: without Hari the sinful demons would not die. He reflected that the Lord had descended to lift earth’s burden, and planned to use this occasion to see his feet, petition him, and bring both brothers. ‘I shall fill my eyes with the Lord who is the abode of knowledge, detachment and every virtue.’ While forming many such hopes, he quickly bathed in the Sarayū and went to the king’s court.",
