    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0207",
      "sequence": 219,
      "meaning": "When the king heard that the sage had come, he went to meet him with an assembly of Brahmins. He prostrated, honored Viśvāmitra, brought him in and seated him on his own seat. Washing his feet and worshipping him deeply, Daśaratha declared that no one was as blessed as he that day, then served him a varied meal that greatly pleased the sage. He next placed all four sons at the sage’s feet. Seeing Rāma, the sage forgot bodily awareness and gazed at his face like a partridge entranced by the full moon. The delighted king said no one had ever shown him such grace and promised to do the purpose of the visit without delay. Viśvāmitra replied that hordes of demons tormented him: he had come as a petitioner for Raghunātha with his younger brother, so that by killing the night-rangers the sage might be protected. He urged the king to give them gladly, abandon delusion and ignorance, and gain righteousness and fame while the boys gained highest welfare.",
