    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0209",
      "sequence": 221,
      "meaning": "Their eyes were reddish, their chests and arms broad; their dark bodies resembled blue lotuses and tamāla trees. Yellow cloth bound their waists, fine quivers were fastened there, and graceful bows and arrows filled both hands. Dark and fair, the beautiful brothers were a great treasure gained by Viśvāmitra. On the road the sage showed them his hermitage and said he knew the Lord as guardian of Brahmins: ‘For my sake, Lord, you left father behind.’ Hearing of Tāṭakā, she charged in fury; with a single arrow Rāma took her life, then, knowing her wretched, granted her his own abode. The seer recognized his Lord in his heart and gave the treasury of knowledge the sacred disciplines by which hunger and thirst do not afflict and strength and bodily radiance become matchless. Offering his whole life, he brought the Lord to his hermitage and served roots and fruit as food, knowing this to aid devotion.",
