    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0210",
      "sequence": 222,
      "meaning": "At dawn Rāma told the sage to go and perform his sacrifice without fear. The companies of seers began the offerings while he himself guarded the rite. Hearing this, the enraged night-ranger Mārīca rushed in with helpers hostile to sages. Rāma struck him with a headless arrow and hurled him a hundred yojanas beyond the sea, then killed Subāhu with a fire-arrow while Lakṣmaṇa destroyed the demonic host. Having slain the demons and made the Brahmins fearless, he received the praise of gods and bands of sages. Raghurāya remained there some days, showing kindness to the Brahmins; for devotion’s sake they told him many ancient stories, though the Lord already knew them. Viśvāmitra then respectfully invited him to witness another event, King Janaka’s bow-sacrifice. Gladly the brothers went with the great sage. On the road the Lord saw a deserted hermitage with no bird, beast or living creature and asked about a stone. The sage told the whole special story: Gautama’s wife, steadfast in a stone-body under his curse, awaited the dust of Rāma’s lotus feet and his grace.",
